children.body:
Reusable world volumes or authored artefacts that alter a controlled set of game rules.

Candidate channels:
- gravity direction / strength;
- object weight or force;
- movement / animation / simulation rate;
- biological growth or state;
- visibility / perception;
- sound filtering / pitch / time;
- fog, light, colour and post processing;
- object / collision state;
- water or environmental motion.

An effect should be authorable as a combination of channels rather than one bespoke code path per puzzle. This gives the design team a versatile 'strangeness vocabulary' while keeping rules consistent.

Support authored interactions with major artefacts without hard-coding each one as a bespoke cutscene.

Likely capabilities:
- trigger / proximity activation;
- state-driven animation and VFX;
- camera takeover and restoration;
- contextual interaction points;
- simple multi-choice or confirm interactions;
- links to world-state / puzzle-state changes;
- audio cues and optional abstract glyph / text presentation;
- save persistence for irreversible mission-level choices.

The final central interface should be built from the same reusable system used for earlier artefact encounters wherever possible.

Each imported concept should eventually store:
- stable concept ID;
- category / biome / subject;
- image asset path;
- original prompt/brief;
- notes / feedback;
- status: Exploration / Shortlisted / Approved Direction / Superseded;
- created/updated date;
- related Design Lab IDs;
- optional lineage/variant link so later iterations can be traced back to the concept they evolved from.
